          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Microlocalization"} Microlocalization
            is a tool to study the propagation of singularities of solutions of partial differential systems, in order to study pullbacks of solutions of differential systems (which generalize products of distributions) and prove
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/index+theorems"} index theorems
          %p
            Derived microlocalization is an adaptation of the theory of microlocalization to the setting of derived
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/global+analytic+geometry"} global analytic geometry
          %h2#motivation_for_a_derived_notion_of_microlocalization Motivation for a derived notion of microlocalization
          %p
            In non-smooth situations, the usual normal and conormal bundle used in classical microlocalization is not well behaved, and one needs to take derived versions of them. Moreover, to study
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/global+analytic+index+theory"} global analytic index theory
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/microlocal+formulation+of+index+theory"} microlocal formulation of index theory
          %h2#loop_space_and_deformation_to_the_normal_bundle_method_i Loop space and deformation to the normal bundle: method I
          %p
            We would like to define a family over (say) the disc
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi D
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D^1
            whose fiber at
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
            is
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi LX
                  %mo :
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Hom
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} LX:=\Hom(S^1,X)
            and whose fiber at
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
            is
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi LX
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %msup
                      %mi S
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [LX/S^1]
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi LX
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} LX
            identifies with
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Hom
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi BG
                      %mi a
                    %mo ,
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Hom(BG_a,X)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi G
                    %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G_a
            act multiplicatively on the action of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi BG
                    %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} BG_a
            on
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi LX
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} LX
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
            and the usual action at
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi BG
                    %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} BG_a
            is the affinization of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                    %mi ℤ
                    %mo ≅
                    %msup
                      %mi S
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B\mathbb{Z}\cong S^1
          %h2#loop_space_and_deformation_to_the_normal_bundle_method_ii Loop space and deformation to the normal bundle: method II
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Microlocalization"} Microlocalization
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+geometry"} derived geometry
            involves the proper definition of the deformation to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/normal+bundle"} normal bundle
            of a closed embedding
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Y
                  %mo ⊂
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y\subset X
            of global analytic spaces (or even stacks). One needs to consider a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+loop+space"} derived loop space
            approach to this construction because it allows to avoid the use of denominators in the definition of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern+character"} Chern character
            (following Connes-Loday-Toen-Vezzosi) and in the development of more general
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/global+analytic+index+theory"} global analytic index theory
            with integral coefficients.
          %p
            The space of paths on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            based on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            is the groupoid acting on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            given by
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi P
                    %mi Y
                  %mi X
                  %mo :
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi f
                  %mo ∈
                  %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Hom
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi Δ
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi Y
                  %mo ,
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P_Y X:=\{f\in \Hom(\Delta^1,X),\;f(0)\in Y,\;f(1)\in Y\}.
          %p More concretely, this is given by the homotopy pullback
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi P
                            %mi Y
                          %mi X
                        %mtd
                          %mo →
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Hom
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %msup
                            %mi Δ
                            %mn 1
                          %mo ,
                          %mi X
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                            %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                              %mrow
                                %msub
                                  %mi ev
                                  %mn 0
                                %mo ×
                                %msub
                                  %mi ev
                                  %mn 1
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi Y
                          %mo ×
                          %mi Y
                        %mtd
                          %mo →
                        %mtd
                          %mi X
                          %mo ×
                          %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  P_Y X &amp;\to&amp; \Hom(\Delta^1,X)
                  \\\
                  \\downarrow &amp;&amp; \downarrow^{\mathrlap{ev_0\times ev_1}}
                  \\\
                  Y\times Y &amp;\to&amp; X\times X
                  }
          %p
            Notice that the natural projection
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi P
                    %mi Y
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi Y
                  %mo ×
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P_Y X\to Y\times Y
            makes
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi P
                    %mi Y
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P_Y X
            a groupoid (of paths in
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                    %mo =
                    %mi M
                    %mo ↪
                    %mi M
                    %mo ×
                    %mi M
                    %mo =
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y=M\hookrightarrow M\times M=X
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi P
                    %mi Y
                  %mi X
                  %mo ≅
                  %mi LM
                  %mo :
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Hom
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mi M
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P_Y X\cong LM:=\Hom(S^1,M).
          %p
            There is a natural projection
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                  %mo :
                  %msub
                    %mi P
                    %mi Y
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Hom
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi Δ
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∼
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p:P_Y X\to \Hom(\Delta^1,X)\sim X
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi P
                    %mi Y
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P_Y X
            is equiped with the natural structure of a groupoid acting on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            through the projection
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi P
                      %mi Y
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi Y
                    %mo ×
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P_Y X\to Y\times Y
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi Δ
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ∼
                    %msup
                      %mi Δ
                      %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta^1\sim \Delta^0
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi P
                    %mi Y
                  %mi X
                  %mo =
                  %mi Y
                  %msubsup
                    %mo ×
                    %mi X
                    %mi h
                  %mi Y
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P_Y X=Y\times^h_X Y,
          %p
            which clearly has an interesting meaning only in the setting of derived geometry. See also at
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+loop+space"} derived loop space
            and at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hochschild+cohomology"} Hochschild cohomology
          %p
            To make the above construction work for general Artin stacks, one needs to make it local for the smooth topology. This is done by replacing the loop space groupoid
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi P
                    %mi Y
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P_Y X
            acting on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            by its formal completion along the identity morphism. This gives a formal groupoid
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mover
                      %mi P
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                    %mi Y
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hat{P}_Y X
            that is equivalent modulo the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/HKR"} HKR
            theorem (in characteristic
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi T
                    %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_Y[-1]X
            for a general Artin stack. Similarly, we will have
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi L
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                  %mi M
                  %mo :
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mover
                      %mi P
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                    %mi M
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi M
                  %mo ×
                  %mi M
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≅
                  %mi T
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hat{L}M:=\hat{P}_M(M\times M)\cong T[-1]M
            by
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/HKR"} HKR
          %p
            The deformation to the normal bundle in strict derived global analytic geometry is then simply given by the (
            %strong> false to be corrected
            ) formula (with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi D
                    %mn 1
                  %mo =
                  %mi 𝕄
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi R
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi X
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mo †
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D^1=\mathbb{M}(R\{X\}^\dagger)
            for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            the base ind-Banach ring)
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi P
                        %mi Y
                      %mi X
                    %mo ˜
                  %mo :
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi f
                  %mo ∈
                  %msub
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Hom
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi D
                        %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi Δ
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ×
                  %msup
                    %mi D
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mi X
                  %mo ×
                  %msup
                    %mi D
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi Y
                  %mo ,
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi Y
                  %mo ,
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
