                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          %h2#a_derived_analog_of_microlocalization A derived analog of microlocalization
          %p
            We now work on an arbitrary derived Artin stack
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Y
                  %mo =
                  %mi M
                  %mo →
                  %mi M
                  %mo ×
                  %mi M
                  %mo =
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y=M\to M\times M=X
            is an affine closed embedding.
          %p
            The usual theory of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/microlocalization"} microlocalization
            may be adapted to the derived global analytic setting by replacing the deformation to the normal bundle
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi T
                        %mi Y
                      %mi X
                    %mo ˜
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mi 𝔸
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \widetilde{T_Y X}\to \mathbb{A}^1
            by the formal deformation to the normal bundle
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi P
                          %mi Y
                        %mi X
                      %mo ^
                    %mo →
                    %msup
                      %mi D
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \widehat{P_Y X}\to D^1
          %p
            The derived specialization functor is given on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo ∈
                  %msup
                    %mi D
                    %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F\in D^b(X)
            by
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ν
                    %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo :
                  %mo =
                  %msup
                    %mi s
                    %mo *
                  %msup
                    %mi p
                    %mo *
                  %mi F
                  %mo ∈
                  %msup
                    %mi D
                    %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mover
                      %mi P
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                    %mi Y
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nu_Y(F):=s^*p^*F\in D^b(\hat{P}_Y X).
          %p
            Remark that the loop space analog
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi L
                    %mo *
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L^*X
            of the odd cotangent bundle
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi T
                    %mo *
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T^*[1]X
            (that should be dual to the odd tangent bundle
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T[-1]X
            (related to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi LX
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} LX
            by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/HKR"} HKR
            theorem) is given by
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^1\otimes X
            (external tensor product by the simplicial circle). We will have for every stack
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Hom
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≅
                  %msub
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Hom
                    %mi SSets
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Hom
                    %mi dSt
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Hom(S^1\otimes X,Y)\cong \Hom_{SSets}(S^1,\Hom_{dSt}(X,Y)).
          %p
            The derived Fourier-Sato transformation is given on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo ∈
                  %msup
                    %mi D
                    %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mover
                      %mi P
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                    %mi Y
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F\in D^b(\hat{P}_Y X)
            by
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Φ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo :
                  %mo =
                  %mo ?
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Phi(F):=?.
          %p
            The derived microlocalization functor is given on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo ∈
                  %msup
                    %mi D
                    %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi M
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F\in D^b(M)
            by
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi μ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo :
                  %mo =
                  %mi Φ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi ν
                    %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu(F):=\Phi(\nu_Y(F))
          %p
            for
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                    %mo =
                    %mi M
                    %mo ↪
                    %mi M
                    %mo ×
                    %mi M
                    %mo =
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y=M\hookrightarrow M\times M=X
          %h2#a_derived_analytic_analog_of_microlocalization A derived analytic analog of microlocalization
          %p
            One may replace the simplicial circle
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi S
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^1
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U(1)
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/overconvergent+global+analytic+geometry"} overconvergent global analytic geometry
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≅
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U(1)\cong S^1
            up to
            = succeed "-homotopy." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi D
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D^1
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi exp
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi i
                  %mo −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo :
                  %mi ℝ
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} exp(i-):\mathbb{R}\to S^1
            also has a meaning in overconvergent complex analytic geometry, if we see
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℝ
                  %mo ⊂
                  %mi ℂ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}\subset \mathbb{C}
            as a closed subset equipped with its germs of analytic functions.
          %p
            In the global analytic setting (without imposing homotopy invariance), there is no reason to have
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %msup
                      %mi D
                      %mn 1
                    %msub
                      %mo ×
                      %mrow
                        %mo *
                        %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∐
                        %mo *
                    %mo *
